response:
A loop is a programming construct that allows you to perform a block of code repeatedly. For example, here is a simple "while" loop that will print each number from 0 to 4 to the console:

while (true) {
    printf("Hello World!\n");
    sleep(2);
}

The "true" value in the first line tells C to always loop, even if the condition becomes false. This is one of the capabilities of "true" - its behavior can be customized, so in general you might want to use something else like false elsewhere in your code.

In this case we're using sleep to add a short delay between iterations of the loop, so the whole thing feels a little more "natural". In general, though, if you're going to be adding some sleep or iteration code, it makes sense to put it where you can access it easily without needing to access any global or static variables - in this case we just have access to "printf".  If the loop runs much more frequently than the iteration / modification time between each iteration, it might make more sense to use global variables.

